 "intro": "By the river Sabis (Sambre), northern Gaul, 57 BC. You are Julius Caesar. The Nervii, the fiercest of the Belgae, are hidden in the woods across the river while your legions build camp.",
 "debrief": "In Caesar’s account, the Nervii charged out of the woods while the legions were digging, and there was no time to form up: each legion fought where it stood. On the left the Ninth and Tenth drove back the Atrebates, but on the right the Twelfth and Seventh were surrounded. Caesar seized a shield, went to the front and rallied them. Labienus, who had taken the enemy camp, sent the Tenth back, and the baggage legions arrived; the Nervii fought to the last, standing on their own dead. Caesar says only 500 of 60,000 survived, probably an exaggeration. The other choices are what-ifs.",
